Here's the implementation of the Template Method design pattern for sorting algorithms:

```java
// Abstract class SortAlgorithm
public abstract class SortAlgorithm {
    public abstract void sort(int[] array);
    public abstract void compare(int[] array);
    public abstract void swap(int[] array, int i, int j);
}

// Concrete class BubbleSort
public class BubbleSort extends SortAlgorithm {
    @Override
    public void sort(int[] array) {
        int n = array.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
                if (compare(array, j) > 0) {
                    swap(array, j, j + 1);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void compare(int[] array) {
        for (int i = 0; i < array.length - 1; i++) {
            if (array[i] > array[i + 1]) {
                return true;
            } else if (array[i] < array[i + 1]) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    @Override
    public void swap(int[] array, int i, int j) {
        int temp = array[i];
        array[i] = array[j];
        array[j] = temp;
    }
}

// Concrete class SelectionSort
public class SelectionSort extends SortAlgorithm {
    @Override
    public void sort(int[] array) {
        for (int i = 0; i < array.length - 1; i++) {
            int minIndex = i;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < array.length; j++) {
                if (compare(array, j) < 0) {
                    minIndex = j;
                }
            }
            if (minIndex!= i) {
                swap(array, i, minIndex);
            }
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void compare(int[] array) {
        for (int i = 0; i < array.length - 1; i++) {
            if (array[i] > array[i + 1]) {
                return true;
            } else if (array[i] < array[i + 1]) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    @Override
    public void swap(int[] array, int i, int j) {
        int temp = array[i];
        array[i] = array[j];
        array[j] = temp;
    }
}

// Main class to test the sorting algorithms
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] array = {5, 2, 8, 3, 1, 6, 4, 7, 9, 0};
        SortAlgorithm bubbleSort = new BubbleSort();
        SortAlgorithm selectionSort = new SelectionSort();

        System.out.println("Original array: " + array);
        bubbleSort.sort(array);
        System.out.println("Sorted array (BubbleSort): " + array);
        System.out.println("Original array: " + array);
        selectionSort.sort(array);
        System.out.println("Sorted array (SelectionSort): " + array);
    }
}
```

This implementation demonstrates the Template Method design pattern for sorting algorithms. The `SortAlgorithm` class serves as a template for different sorting algorithms, and the `BubbleSort` and `SelectionSort` classes provide specific implementations for the `sort()` method. The `compare()` and `swap()` methods are abstract and must be implemented by the concrete subclasses. The `Main` class tests the sorting algorithms by creating instances of `BubbleSort` and `SelectionSort` and sorting arrays of integers.
